## Groups, wrappers and real contexts

| Generated context | Groups / classes |
| --- | --- |
| `AuditOtp001.Examples.v2.consumer` | `ConsumerExamples`: Discovery, UpdateRetainCode, ProvisionHmac, CalculateHmac, AesTicket, StaticPassword, SwapDelete, PromptSession, BorrowedConnectionProvision, ExistingSessionChallenge, CachedState, MigrationCurrentV2, SharedOperationWording, InlineFixedChallenge, InlineConfigured, InlineReadState, InlineHidOptions. `SharedIntentPrompt` is a type-only group implemented by the real `IntentPrompt : IUserPresencePrompt`; the original README `TouchPrompt` type is also retained. |
| `AuditOtp001.Examples.v2.annotations` | InlineTrait: original `[Trait(TestCategories.Category, TestCategories.RequiresUserPresence)]` on `AnnotationExample`, using actual xUnit-v2 and Tests.Shared constants. This is a compilation-only attribute declaration, **not** a placeholder test or a device probe. No Fact/Theory or test execution is invented. |
| Actual shipping `OtpTool.csproj` | All original sample classes/top-level program, original references/global imports and existing analyzer policy. No source body is replaced. Its existing CA1852 CLI/example waiver is retained as existing project policy, not added by these hosts. |

Consumer hosts reference the real YubiOtp project, bringing its actual Core public dependency
transitively. Annotation compilation uses the genuine YubiOtp integration project's complete
central-package reference elements and a real Tests.Shared project reference. All compiler
hosts import canonical root props/targets and keep warnings-as-errors and analyzers enabled.
They are unique `Yubico.YubiKit.AuditOtp001.Examples.*.csproj` projects, not solution members;
normal solution compilation does not automatically qualify these snippets.

`raw/*.cs.txt` retains complete original bodies. Typed wrappers provide actual `IYubiKey`,
`IConnection`, `YubiOtpSession`, `ConfigState`, owned byte arrays, callback contexts and
cancellation-token prerequisites rather than dummy implementations. Only namespace import
directives are separated into file scope. `using`, `await using`, braces, `try/finally`, input
clearing and owned-output cleanup remain intact. Prompt types are placed at namespace scope
instead of illegally preceding top-level statements. The generic wording wrapper returns the
original `message`; type-only callback grouping adds no empty callable placeholder.

The four inline expression groups bind configured-state access, one-shot state read, fixed
challenge mode and explicit HID session options. Unqualified instance-method designators
receive a recorded real typed receiver; no client/API is simulated. A borrowed configuration
or session stays caller-owned. Current task groups retain their actual destructive effects,
access-code/update prerequisites, verifier-enrollment responsibility and existing-session
lifetime. Compilation does not supply missing application enrollment/verifier policy.

## Shipping sample discovery and capture

Static inspection found that toolchain build discovery requires a `Yubico.YubiKit.` path
substring. The shipping `src/YubiOtp/examples/OtpTool/OtpTool.csproj` lacks it, so plain
selector `OtpTool` otherwise has no buildable match in this checkout. No toolchain or
shipping project was edited to conceal that limitation.

The prepared, **unbuilt** traversal driver is
`validation/examples/dispatch/src/Yubico.YubiKit.AuditOtp001.Examples.Shipping.OtpTool.csproj`.
It is the one prefix-qualified `OtpTool` selector match and delegates Restore/Clean/Build/
Rebuild directly to the **untouched actual shipping project** with the toolchain's selected
configuration. It is an MSBuild traversal file, not a C# host, clone, fake API or reduced
sample. The actual SDK project still imports canonical props/targets and resolves all real
references/packages. The coordinator must accept and validate this driver before execution;
no driver/build success is claimed here.

Two process-scoped external hooks are supplied for that actual project:

- `v2/otptool/OtpTool.Output.props`, via `CustomBeforeMicrosoftCommonProps`, changes only the
  exact `MSBuildProjectName == OtpTool` project's output/intermediate paths early enough.
- `v2/otptool/OtpTool.Capture.targets`, via `CustomAfterMicrosoftCommonTargets`, observes actual
  Csc inputs/policy and enables command-line argument capture for that same exact project.

These hooks do not replace canonical Directory.Build imports, affect another module's
compiler policy or change source/reference/analyzer selection. They do not execute the CLI.
All new host/sample outputs and capture files go to
`/tmp/opencode/yubiotp-001-examples-v2-{consumer,annotations,otptool}`. Referenced SDK/shared
projects keep their canonical build context; no binaries/cache are copied into run evidence.
The MSBuild hook/import-order mechanism was checked against current Microsoft Learn docs
after a Context7 lookup; the evaluated behavior is still coordinator-build evidence, not a
static-preparation result.

## Exact coordinator commands — not executed here

Run from the repository root, serially with every other toolchain/device operation. Read
the RESULT/exit status and raw compiler/analyzer diagnostics after each build. Do not use
`test`, `run`, sample launch commands or hardware operations for this assignment.

```bash
ROOT=/home/rupicola/Code/y/Yubico.Yubikit.NET
RUN=docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/yubiotp/pass-1/yubiotp-001
EX="$RUN/validation/examples"

python3 -B "$EX/verify.py" v2 prepared

python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-consumer-build dotnet toolchain.cs -- build --project AuditOtp001.Examples.v2.consumer --clean
python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-consumer-capture python3 -B "$EX/capture.py" v2 consumer first

python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-annotations-build dotnet toolchain.cs -- build --project AuditOtp001.Examples.v2.annotations --clean
python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-annotations-capture python3 -B "$EX/capture.py" v2 annotations first

python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-otptool-build env CustomBeforeMicrosoftCommonProps="$ROOT/$EX/v2/otptool/OtpTool.Output.props" CustomAfterMicrosoftCommonTargets="$ROOT/$EX/v2/otptool/OtpTool.Capture.targets" dotnet toolchain.cs -- build --project OtpTool --clean
python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-otptool-capture python3 -B "$EX/capture.py" v2 otptool first

python3 "$RUN/validation/run-command.py" examples-v2-final-verify python3 -B "$EX/verify.py" v2 compiled --capture consumer=first --capture annotations=first --capture otptool=first --live
```

The build commands retain real toolchain logs before five-run rotation, including raw
warnings/errors and failures. Capture immediately after the associated build, before another
build overwrites temporary capture files. A capture pass is **not** a build-success verdict:
the actual matching build must separately report zero compiler/analyzer warnings and errors.
Pre-compile target output can survive a failed compile even when post-compile policy/argument
files are missing; capture failures preserve available text plus `capture-failure.json` and
must not be counted as compiler/example failures or successes without the real build log.

Each context/attempt capture directory is exclusive-create. Retry with new log and attempt
names; never overwrite `first` or regenerate a prior extraction. Any required body/wrapper/
context changes need a **new extraction revision** and new compile/capture evidence. Do not
remove assertions, original resource scopes or diagnostics to force a green result. Stop and
record an actual content/API/context issue when the real compiler reveals one.

## Evidence and final reconciliation

The generator adapts frozen Security Domain host props/targets and compiler-capture approach,
not old example bodies or results. `v2/manifest.json` pins exact source/target template hashes,
substitutions/transforms, canonical source/configuration dependencies, the correction-record
hash, raw/full wrapper bodies and every generated source/config file. Version-local
`tool-source/*.snapshot` retains the exact generator/inventory/capture/verifier sources.

Actual capture checks **Csc arguments against the full source, resolved-reference and analyzer
item sets**, requires canonical .NET/xUnit analyzers in their respective contexts, and retains
the original generated source, actual source snapshots, analyzer configs, evaluated policy,
actual `project.assets.json`, root props/targets/package/global/editor configuration and
compiled project. Binary reference/analyzer paths, sizes and hashes are retained without
copying those files. For OtpTool, membership must include every one of its fourteen real
sample source files; a successfully built traversal file alone is insufficient.

Final verification rescans current documents and all module XML documentation. It detects
changed/new/removed C# fences, meaningful inline expressions, XML code/spans and current
mapping signatures; it checks exact raw/wrapper bodies and prepared source/config/dependency
hashes. It permits surrounding Markdown/YAML prose and line-number shifts when compiled
bodies/signatures stay unchanged, printing current document hashes rather than silently
rebasing the original snapshot. C# source/config dependency drift is deliberately stricter.
With compiled capture selectors it also binds the retained actual compiler source snapshots
to the final current host/sample bodies and canonical config. `--live` rechecks available
current binary/analyzer/assets identities before closeout; omit it only for explicitly
offline retained-archive verification, not to imply current dependencies were checked.

Version `v1/` and its logs remain unchanged as the initial successful **preparation-only**
checkpoint. It already contained REV003 but did not yet solve shipping-sample discovery and
isolated output capture. `v2/` supersedes it for coordinator execution. No compile evidence
exists for either revision at this handoff. Root utility sources now describe v2; v1's exact
original sources remain archived under its own `tool-source/`.

